<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    DEVELOPER VERIFICATION TOOL — NOT PART OF THE PRODUCT. See tools/probes/README.md.

    Data Import — etap I0 (docs/design/data-import.md §6). Measures the WRITE path against a live engine,
    so three design claims rest on measurement instead of inference:

      (W) throughput — prepared INSERT + re-bind vs FbBatchCommand vs the naive per-row command; the cost
          of committing every N rows (risk R7 / the Batched transaction mode).
      (B) does a BATCH tell us WHICH row failed? Without that, the batch path cannot fill the error report
          (§3.7) and loses on correctness regardless of speed — the blocking question for R7.
      (T) is an over-long string an ERROR or a silent truncation? (§0.2 — the answer decides whether the
          "trim too long values" option is even needed as a guard.)
      (C) the connection CHARSET question (risk R1): when a value carries a character the connection charset
          cannot represent, does it fail — and at WHICH layer (client or server) — or is it silently
          substituted? A silent substitution is a §0 data-corruption vector no server error would report.

    Raw driver only — no Core/Firebird reference: at I0 no production import code exists, and this measures
    the ENGINE + DRIVER, exactly as Fb3ClosureProbe measures the engine rather than the interpreter.

    Deliberately NOT in EmberTern.slnx (explicit <Project> entries, no globs): it needs a live server and a
    password, which the hermetic test suite must never need. Creates and DROPS a throwaway scratch database
    at an ASCII path — it never touches Lab/EmberTern_Lab.fdb.

    How to run: see the header of Program.cs.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="FirebirdSql.Data.FirebirdClient" Version="10.3.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
